WEBVTT

0
00:00:04.070 --> 00:00:06.010
Welcome to module 5.

1
00:00:06.630 --> 00:00:11.609
We have covered the physical and geographic boundaries of AI infrastructure.

2
00:00:12.109 --> 00:00:14.810
Now we must look at how we actually control it.

3
00:00:15.399 --> 00:00:17.600
In a standard cloud environment,

4
00:00:17.790 --> 00:00:19.659
automation is straightforward.

5
00:00:20.420 --> 00:00:22.299
If a virtual machine fails,

6
00:00:22.540 --> 00:00:25.600
your orchestration layer simply spins up a new one.

7
00:00:25.940 --> 00:00:28.579
The overall application barely notices.

8
00:00:29.790 --> 00:00:31.399
But in distributed AI

9
00:00:31.790 --> 00:00:36.650
infrastructure orchestration introduces massive coordination overhead.

10
00:00:37.110 --> 00:00:39.840
You are not just managing independent servers,

11
00:00:40.029 --> 00:00:42.569
you are managing a highly coordinated,

12
00:00:42.750 --> 00:00:44.330
distributed system.

13
00:00:44.750 --> 00:00:47.330
Let us look closely at automated failover.

14
00:00:47.709 --> 00:00:51.110
If a single GPU fails during a massive training run,

15
00:00:51.349 --> 00:00:55.209
the orchestrator cannot just swap in a replacement in the background.

16
00:00:55.720 --> 00:00:57.380
Depending on architecture,

17
00:00:57.639 --> 00:01:01.119
the orchestrator may need to pause coordinated workloads,

18
00:01:01.560 --> 00:01:03.099
replace resources,

19
00:01:03.400 --> 00:01:04.470
restore state,

20
00:01:04.720 --> 00:01:06.440
and resume execution.

21
00:01:07.129 --> 00:01:09.309
It must eject the failed node,

22
00:01:09.650 --> 00:01:10.949
provision a new one,

23
00:01:11.239 --> 00:01:13.029
verify the system state,

24
00:01:13.279 --> 00:01:15.489
and then force thousands of GPUs to

25
00:01:15.489 --> 00:01:18.569
roll back to their last synchronized checkpoint.

26
00:01:19.980 --> 00:01:23.050
This process creates intense complexity.

27
00:01:23.400 --> 00:01:28.050
If your automation systems are not perfectly tuned to handle distributed state,

28
00:01:28.330 --> 00:01:32.169
a single failover event can trigger network congestion,

29
00:01:32.489 --> 00:01:33.830
recovery delays,

30
00:01:34.089 --> 00:01:36.650
or broader coordination failures.

31
00:01:38.629 --> 00:01:43.930
Automation at AI scale becomes a first-class architectural concern.

32
00:01:44.400 --> 00:01:46.800
You must design orchestration logic

33
00:01:46.959 --> 00:01:50.949
that respects the heavy physical cost of system coordination.